When `os.unlink` fails (permissions, read-only file, race with a process holding the file), the `except` swallows the error and execution still falls through to `freed_bytes += ...`. The function therefore reports bytes as "freed" that were never freed — exactly the kind of ignored failure status the return value is supposed to reflect. The increment belongs inside the `try` (after the unlink) or in an `else:` block. For a cron job whose return value feeds monitoring/reporting, this silently wrong number matters. Relatedly, all failures (stat, unlink, scandir) are swallowed with no logging, so a broken nightly run produces no signal at all.

